/**
@page setup Setup

@brief Initialise a project for a new site, and instantiate the library for it.

A **site** is the single source of truth for one greenhouse: its PLC payload plus
the declarative tables that describe it (status variables, home-screen readings,
shell commands, config parameters, alarms). `libbotanik` stays generic - a site is
data, not code. Starting a new deployment means creating a site directory and
pointing the build at it, copying the reference site @ref reference "gh_mlb41" as a
template.

@section setup_clone Getting the code

The library is meant to be consumed as a git submodule of the project (or vendored
in-tree). To work on it directly, clone it:

@code{.sh}
git clone git@framagit.org:pstch2/botanik.git
cd botanik
@endcode

The Linux simulator builds with just CMake and a C++11 compiler (see @ref targets).
The hardware build also needs the Arduino Mbed OPTA core (auto-discovered by the CMake
OPTA build); install it once with arduino-cli:

@code{.sh}
arduino-cli core install arduino:mbed_opta   # one-time: the cross-toolchain for OPTA
@endcode

@section setup_dir The site directory

Create `src/sites/<name>/` (the `<name>` is the code-name you select with
`-DBOTANIK_SITE`, e.g. `gh_mlb41`). A site is a small, fixed set of files:

| File | Holds |
|------|-------|
| `site.h` | The PLC payload (`PlcInputData_t` / `PlcOutputData_t` plus its status enums and their `str()` companions), the declarative tables (status-variable `ScalarGroup[]`, home-field `HomeField[]`, shell `ShellCommand[]`, config `ConfigParam[]`), the `SiteDescriptor` (`g_site`) that bundles them, and the thin firmware binding. It exposes the uniform handle a harness builds against: `BotanikSite`, the payload types, and the sim-only mock-PLC entry. |
| `alarms.h` | The `g_alarms[]` table - one row per alarm: code, description, the byte `offsetof` of that alarm's boolean field in `PlcOutputData_t`, dwell policy, and a remediation note. |
| `sim.h` | The header-only mock PLC: the data animator that fills the payload with plausible values for the simulator. Its body is guarded by `#ifdef TARGET_SIMULATOR`, so it drops out of the hardware build. |
| `config.h` | Build-wide config overrides: `#define BOTANIK_*` for any value in `libbotanik_config.h` this site needs to pin (the `LOG_PLC_*` sizes must match the PLC program). Kept separate so CMake can include it before the library defaults. |

The site declares **no pages** and overrides **no lifecycle hooks** - the base owns
every page and wires every table (see @ref setup_descriptor). The declarative tables
are meant to be generated from the Arduino PLC project.

@section setup_payload The payload contract

The site's `PlcInputData_t` / `PlcOutputData_t` is the byte-for-byte layout shared with
the M4 coprocessor (the PLC IDE generates the matching type on its side). Both the
simulator's mock PLC and the OPTA firmware use it verbatim - never fork it per target.
The declarative tables address the payload **by byte offset**: each status variable
and each alarm names a field in `PlcOutputData_t` (an alarm names its own `uint8_t`
boolean - there is no packed alarm bitset). The site defines its layout to satisfy
those contracts and verifies the size with `static_assert`s.

@section setup_descriptor Declaring the site: the SiteDescriptor

Everything site-specific is gathered into one `Botanik::SiteDescriptor` (see
`core/firmware.h`): the site name plus the generated tables. Any table may be
null / count 0.

@code{.cpp}
const Botanik::SiteDescriptor g_site = {
    Botanik::Config::SITE_NAME,
    g_params,   g_param_count,          // config parameters (CONFIG page + `config` cmd)
    g_commands, g_command_count,        // site shell commands
    g_status,   g_status_group_count,   // status variables (MONITOR page, telemetry, `status`)
    g_home,     g_home_field_count,     // home-screen readings (up to 5 left + 8 right)
    g_alarms,   g_alarm_count,          // alarm conditions
};
@endcode

The base `BotanikFirmware` reads this descriptor to build and wire every page
(home / shell / logs / alarms / config / monitor) and every backend generically -
no per-field code, and no per-site page classes.

@section setup_firmware Instantiating the firmware

A site is a thin binding that hands the descriptor to the base:

@code{.cpp}
class GreenhouseXXX final : public Botanik::BotanikFirmware<PlcInputData_t, PlcOutputData_t> {
public:
    GreenhouseXXX(Botanik::PlcIO::PlcInput<PlcInputData_t>*   in,
                  Botanik::PlcIO::PlcOutput<PlcOutputData_t>* out)
        : Botanik::BotanikFirmware<PlcInputData_t, PlcOutputData_t>(in, out, g_site) {}

    GreenhouseXXX() : GreenhouseXXX(hal_input(), hal_output()) {}   // bind the HAL region
};
using BotanikSite = GreenhouseXXX;
@endcode

That is the whole firmware class: it declares no members and overrides nothing. A site
that genuinely needs custom per-beat logic or an extra page (for example re-adding a
`StatusPage`) may still override the base's `on_step()` / `register_pages()` hooks here,
but the common case needs neither.

Both targets instantiate the same `BotanikSite`, differing only in where the PLC region
comes from:

- **Simulator** (`botanik_sim/main.cpp`): allocates the shared region as local statics
  and passes them to the explicit-region constructor, then drives a mock coprocessor
  from the site's `sim.h` animator.
- **Hardware** (the Arduino entry point): uses the default constructor, which binds to
  the coprocessor's region via `HAL::PlcIO::input()` / `output()`.
  for how the sketch, the project-root wiring, and the imported library fit together.

@section setup_build Selecting the site

The site is chosen at configure time; CMake globs `src/sites/<name>/` and points
`BOTANIK_SITE_HEADER` at its `site.h`, so the harnesses never name the site directly:

@code{.sh}
cmake -B build -DBOTANIK_TARGET=SIMULATOR -DBOTANIK_SITE=<name>
make -C build botanik_sim
@endcode

With the site building and animating under the simulator, the next step is wiring it
into the hardware project .
*/
